[{:db/id "q-132982/field-1/f99d61ec370389a0436bb23e63a512bcf60f8c4d8aedf04509d0c5f5026e65a3" :answer/id #uuid "2aab430c-1b17-5cc1-90a4-8d41fc77cf44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\ln(2t^2+3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132982/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6f2d34d-83f5-5f35-94b6-f140f83d8b69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-132982/field-1/f99d61ec370389a0436bb23e63a512bcf60f8c4d8aedf04509d0c5f5026e65a3" :answer-field/choices ["q-132982/field-1/f99d61ec370389a0436bb23e63a512bcf60f8c4d8aedf04509d0c5f5026e65a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132982" :question/id #uuid "8dde8f5a-3e91-5ae3-8e96-278c6ed4f8e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-132982" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nThe particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{t(2t^2+3)}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second equals\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nThe acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{66}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$\n\nEarlier part 8:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{At+B}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t},\n$$\n\nfind $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-16$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,C=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\int \\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,K.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2{t}^{2}+3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can solve the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-16\\int \\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\cdot t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+K \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-132982/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18ce1807-3a09-5ebd-b3cd-a91db8d2b4a8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132982"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132976/field-1/a639343e5acb696f418f98baa0b83fcdf2842d5bad77bd5623d586dd6d16cda7" :answer/id #uuid "e4c276cc-aeed-5362-acbf-ff4efe755cde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{125}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132976/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3b94854-0842-5e48-8133-ccbf5875df06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-132976/field-1/a639343e5acb696f418f98baa0b83fcdf2842d5bad77bd5623d586dd6d16cda7" :answer-field/choices ["q-132976/field-1/a639343e5acb696f418f98baa0b83fcdf2842d5bad77bd5623d586dd6d16cda7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132976" :question/id #uuid "9f987e35-1a6f-5179-a128-776cd6f81c42" :question/math-academy-id "q-132976" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is equal to ${x}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn part 2, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{6-12(1)^{2}}{(2(1)^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is $-\\frac{6}{125}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-132976/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132976"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132977/field-1/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9" :answer/id #uuid "fc4033c6-2524-5144-a1be-2381e92ae647" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132977/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "885f525a-e981-55f4-ba11-5ea31b1e9516" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-132977/field-1/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9" :answer-field/choices ["q-132977/field-1/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132977" :question/id #uuid "96f735fc-ef04-5f32-b866-b00213d2686a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132977" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $x(t)$ is a point $t=c$ in the domain of $x$ such that either ${x}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${x}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nIn part 2, we obtained\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${x}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ 6-12{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 6 & =12{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $x(t)$ is only defined for $t>0.$ Therefore, $x(t)$ has a stationary point at $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n* Second, we note that ${x}^{′}(t)$ exists for all values of $t$ in the domain of $x(t).$ So $x(t)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $x(t)$ is $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-132977/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132977"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132980/field-1/3369e21a231ef35478eb5abed1cc94f66363affcb4761f44425ab7fee73aa67d" :answer/id #uuid "6e1b1660-a9da-5b62-a5ed-7ec0c0144b6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{66}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132980/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b451575f-0689-5c30-afb2-7382a01bd184" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-132980/field-1/3369e21a231ef35478eb5abed1cc94f66363affcb4761f44425ab7fee73aa67d" :answer-field/choices ["q-132980/field-1/3369e21a231ef35478eb5abed1cc94f66363affcb4761f44425ab7fee73aa67d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132980" :question/id #uuid "58e30588-c9a1-5704-9feb-72126881f4ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-132980" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nThe particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{t(2t^2+3)}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second equals\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity in the $y$-direction is given by ${y}^{′}(t).$ Therefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction is ${y}^{″}(t):$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}^{′}(t)$ using the quotient rule, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-24t)(2{t}^{3}+3t)-(6-12{t}^{2})(6{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-48{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-36{t}^{2}-18+72{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-18}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(4{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}-3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{6(4(1)^{4}-12(1)^{2}-3)}{(2(1)^{3}+3(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-11)}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{66}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is $-\\frac{66}{25}\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-132980/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0cdcbc5-c10b-5dd1-af9a-410d2d44a4c5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132980"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132978/field-1/32b9a5eb108e48c3c2f28dd0cae6b8af8d8cf383adf4a1b828b0ceed57ec4a1b" :answer/id #uuid "b93f04b3-4ce2-5194-99b0-d9bd09680c25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6-12t^2}{t(2t^2+3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132978/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "517735cb-10ed-594d-822e-271805774976" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-132978/field-1/32b9a5eb108e48c3c2f28dd0cae6b8af8d8cf383adf4a1b828b0ceed57ec4a1b" :answer-field/choices ["q-132978/field-1/32b9a5eb108e48c3c2f28dd0cae6b8af8d8cf383adf4a1b828b0ceed57ec4a1b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132978" :question/id #uuid "0e21b36e-0c82-5fdb-add8-811f9e999707" :question/math-academy-id "q-132978" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into our expression for ${y}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot 2{(\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-132978/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b47a8b7-722d-564e-8e7a-ad07577d6f6b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132978"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132979/field-1/19d5466e84349f416e7f242f3eae0e8ce9f2558634cf0796beb62f8e60f5ea80" :answer/id #uuid "c7e47667-e60c-5c19-8ca8-6e97bbe362f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132979/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f68117e6-afb3-54c9-948d-96ff4e220af7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-132979/field-1/19d5466e84349f416e7f242f3eae0e8ce9f2558634cf0796beb62f8e60f5ea80" :answer-field/choices ["q-132979/field-1/19d5466e84349f416e7f242f3eae0e8ce9f2558634cf0796beb62f8e60f5ea80"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132979" :question/id #uuid "e48b6023-b414-5092-a3cf-aaf0912c2bdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132979" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nThe particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{t(2t^2+3)}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second equals\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate when $t=1$ is given by ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn step 5, we found that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{6-12(1)^{2}}{2(1)^{3}+3(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12}{2+3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-132979/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b47a8b7-722d-564e-8e7a-ad07577d6f6b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132979"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132981/field-1/4644593a6ae896793c595e8a37cd468ba91be6a8038e715c4c88cddad1968793" :answer/id #uuid "b32cf114-3776-5d87-b8f1-45724b07c4fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132981/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e914ebb-4909-54e4-a43a-b91afa5be585" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-132981/field-1/4644593a6ae896793c595e8a37cd468ba91be6a8038e715c4c88cddad1968793" :answer-field/choices ["q-132981/field-1/4644593a6ae896793c595e8a37cd468ba91be6a8038e715c4c88cddad1968793"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132981/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "f54a9bf9-2243-5679-af1b-99aa22f69d90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132981/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "7960cc18-14e1-5d8c-ba03-57724d376b0b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-132981/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-132981/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132981/field-3/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "479f24e1-04b6-505a-94b2-a49a6a116868" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132981/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd747018-fa2c-5cbd-89e1-13b605a73f00" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-132981/field-3/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-132981/field-3/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132981" :question/id #uuid "53affe2a-22ef-5e1b-8c8f-75fb6de4344f" :question/math-academy-id "q-132981" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nThe particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{t(2t^2+3)}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second equals\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nThe acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{66}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{At+B}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t},\n$$\n\nfind $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,C=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)}=\\frac{At+B}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t}\n$$\n\nNote that since $p(t)=2{t}^{2}+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $At+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $t(2{t}^{2}+3)$ and get\n\n$$\n6-12{t}^{2}=(At+B)t+C(2{t}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=0$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-12(0)^{2} & =(A(0)+B)(0)+C(2(0)^{2}+3) \\\\ 6 & =3C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-12(1)^{2} & =(A(1)+B)(1)+(2)(2(1)^{2}+3) \\\\ -6 & =A+B+10 \\\\ A+B & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=-1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-12(-1)^{2} & =(A(-1)+B)(-1)+(2)(2(-1)^{2}+3) \\\\ -6 & =A-B+10 \\\\ A-B & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-16, \\\\ A-B=-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution of this system is $A=-16$ and $B=0.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)} & =\\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{2}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-132981/field-1" "q-132981/field-2" "q-132981/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132981"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132974/field-1/aa93311fc539c7248ba7da72d76da24598eaaa2420ce8a4f44747c211af68486" :answer/id #uuid "1593f063-3246-54e5-8621-ba9abad3c944" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16t^3+24t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132974/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d7881a1e-264b-54f5-92b8-6669fea0f773" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-132974/field-1/aa93311fc539c7248ba7da72d76da24598eaaa2420ce8a4f44747c211af68486" :answer-field/choices ["q-132974/field-1/aa93311fc539c7248ba7da72d76da24598eaaa2420ce8a4f44747c211af68486"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132974" :question/id #uuid "6a320cd6-807e-5997-8b65-5fa33e189660" :question/math-academy-id "q-132974" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)=2{t}^{2}+3,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =(2{t}^{2}+3)^{′}=4t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-132974/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132974"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132975/field-1/863c952ac6f5ceb19a4b8474067429a9811bc0c17ec937f03b4a43e08eb4ad05" :answer/id #uuid "f468ae22-6b17-56e6-b319-588500df770a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132975/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d5506a20-9f8a-52fe-abfc-e942cce02522" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-132975/field-1/863c952ac6f5ceb19a4b8474067429a9811bc0c17ec937f03b4a43e08eb4ad05" :answer-field/choices ["q-132975/field-1/863c952ac6f5ceb19a4b8474067429a9811bc0c17ec937f03b4a43e08eb4ad05"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132975" :question/id #uuid "99412c9e-d222-55f7-9078-6dcf7bb7d499" :question/math-academy-id "q-132975" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate the function\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwe use the quotient rule with the numerator $g(t)=2t$ and the denominator $h(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating $g(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=2,\n$$\n\nand from part 1, we have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(t)=8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)-g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t)}{[h(t)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)\\cdot (2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}-(2t)\\cdot (8t(2{t}^{2}+3))}{{((2{t}^{2}+3)^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}-16{t}^{2}(2{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)-16{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{2}+6-16{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-132975/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132975"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132983/field-1/4d91afeb69d36c1c675dd1e394f56cd3b867df0240c853a31d352cccef40f7df" :answer/id #uuid "f166218b-accf-568b-a67c-c09a8b2d0abc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{t^2}{(2t^2+3)^4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132983/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c7f7fb0-6124-585d-b7ee-b3e18382e87f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-132983/field-1/4d91afeb69d36c1c675dd1e394f56cd3b867df0240c853a31d352cccef40f7df" :answer-field/choices ["q-132983/field-1/4d91afeb69d36c1c675dd1e394f56cd3b867df0240c853a31d352cccef40f7df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132983" :question/id #uuid "5eeeaf73-5eae-5c4f-8c30-fb9e55938f4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-132983" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nThe particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{t(2t^2+3)}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second equals\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nThe acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{66}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$\n\nEarlier part 8:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{At+B}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t},\n$$\n\nfind $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-16$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,C=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nEarlier part 9:\n\n$\\int \\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\ln(2t^2+3)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,K.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{t}^{2}+3)^{4})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}})+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-132983/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a58c0a18-4221-50f1-b31c-ae58d099d79d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132983"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
